**Reading 1: wrapper over `malloc`.** Over-allocate by `align - 1 + sizeof(void*)`, round up, and keep the original pointer just before the aligned address:

```c
void *amalloc(size_t size, size_t align) {
    if (!align || (align & (align - 1))) return NULL;   // power of two only
    if (align < sizeof(void *)) align = sizeof(void *);
    if (size > SIZE_MAX - align - sizeof(void *)) return NULL; // overflow guard
    void *raw = malloc(size + align + sizeof(void *));
    if (!raw) return NULL;
    uintptr_t a = ((uintptr_t)raw + sizeof(void *) + align - 1) & ~(uintptr_t)(align - 1);
    ((void **)a)[-1] = raw;
    return (void *)a;
}
void afree(void *p) { if (p) free(((void **)p)[-1]); }
```

The mask `~(align - 1)` clears the low bits, and adding `align - 1` first rounds up instead of down. Waste is bounded by `align + sizeof(void*)` bytes per block.

**Reading 2: inside your own allocator (pool or bump arena).** Keep a cursor into a big buffer and round the cursor up to `align` before handing out a block. For free-list allocators, store a small header (size, flags) immediately before the aligned address, and when the padding needed is larger than a minimum block, return the leading gap to the free list so it is not wasted.

**Testing.** Assert `((uintptr_t)p % align) == 0` for several alignments (16, 64, 4096), sizes (0, 1, large) and many allocations, and run under AddressSanitizer to check the stored pointer is never overwritten.

**Prefer the library when possible.** `posix_memalign`, C11 `aligned_alloc` (size must be a multiple of alignment), `_aligned_malloc` on Windows, `mmap` for page alignment. Why alignment matters: SIMD loads, DMA and GPU buffers, cache-line isolation against false sharing.
